            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Moral conduct, the path of life, readiness to spread the Gospel, and humility or servanthood (particularly in the context of foot-washing).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Psalm 119:105\",\"Isaiah 52:7\",\"Romans 10:15\",\"Ephesians 6:15\",\"John 13:1-17\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Authority, dominion, and total subjugation of enemies (when things are placed 'under the feet').\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 8:6\",\"Joshua 10:24\",\"1 Corinthians 15:25-27\",\"Ephesians 1:22\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A Hebrew euphemism for genitalia, sexual intimacy, or private parts.\",\"basis\":[\"Ruth 3:4-14\",\"Isaiah 7:20\",\"Exodus 4:25\",\"1 Samuel 24:3\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Judgment, contempt, or destructive oppression (when trampling underfoot).\",\"basis\":[\"Luke 21:24\",\"Hebrews 10:29\",\"Matthew 7:6\",\"Malachi 4:3\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolism of feet in the Bible is highly contextual and generally tied to an associated action. Washing feet universally symbolizes humility and spiritual cleansing; beautiful or shod feet denote the spreading of the Gospel; and placing something under the feet denotes supreme authority. In academic biblical scholarship, 'feet' is also widely recognized as an Old Testament euphemism for reproductive organs.\"}",
